the University of Toronto,

00:01:18.415 --> 00:01:20.610
which is dedicated to
the research excavation

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.840
and publication of early

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.923
Theravada Buddhist archeology at Angkor.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.470
Dr. Harris is a previous

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.170
recipient of the The Robert
HN Ho Dissertation Fellowship

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.460
in Buddhist Studies

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:33.810
and has published his work

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.720
in World Archeology in Medieval Worlds,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.846
with upcoming publications
and (indistinct)

00:01:38.846 --> 00:01:40.897
ACA, Asian archeology,

00:01:40.897 --> 00:01:43.620
and the Brill handbook in memory studies,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.430
which we just heard about briefly

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.083
from my colleague Wang Chung.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.680
I have much more to
say about Andrew's work

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.500
and how excited I am to have him here,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.380
but nobody came here to listen to me.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.910
So I'm gonna pause here

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.810
and just ask you all to join me

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.444
as we welcome Dr. Andrew
Harris to our virtual series.

00:02:01.444 --> 00:02:03.537
His talk is entitled,

00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:07.220
"The Introduction of Theravada
Buddhism to Angkor, Cambodia,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.970
circa 13th through 16th centuries,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.630
mapping and interpreting religious change

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.370
through the archeological record."

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.841
So please join me in welcoming Dr. Harris.

00:02:16.841 --> 00:02:19.900
(Erik clapping)

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.530
- So I wanna thank, of course,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.410
the Center for Southeast Asian Studies

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.880
for inviting me to their
Brownbag Seminar Series.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.713
It is a great honor

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:29.500
to be able to present all in front of you.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.860
As Erik mentioned, my
name is Andrew Harris.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:33.920
I've never been called doctor

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.630
so many times in a row at this point.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.080
So I'm very happy about that.

00:02:38.023 --> 00:02:39.460
I'm a Research Associate

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.130
at the Archeology Center of
the University of Toronto,

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.780
and I'm also a sessional lecturer

00:02:44.903 --> 00:02:45.736
at Trent University, Durham.

00:02:46.896 --> 00:02:48.550
So before I get started,

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.840
I want to acknowledge several people,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.430
organizations and funding bodies

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:56.530
who have been integral to
getting my research completed

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.500
and completing my dissertation.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.890
Especially those members of
APSARA National Authority

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.130
who have been generous
with granting us permits

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.110
and rights to excavate the incredible

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.883
archeological record
of Cambodia and Angkor.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.970
So today's talk is
primarily going to cover,

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.230
as the title suggests,

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.420
the religious transition and
transformation of Angkor,

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.840
and notably, Angkor Thom in Cambodia.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.510
And we're gonna cover
a few distinct topics.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.090
First, starting with religion, statecraft,

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.340
and temple cosmology,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.300
and what is often an acronymstickly

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.800
known as "Indianized"
Cambodia and Southeast Asia.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.720
I'm gonna cover a little
bit about Angkor Thom,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.680
the theater of early Theravada Buddhist

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.660
place-making at Angkor,

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.410
what entails Buddhist terrace archeology

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.230
and Theravada monastic
construction at Angkor.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.890
Then we'll talk about converted temples,

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.570
which are the major evidence

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.650
for evolving perceptions
of past religious spaces.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.690
Then I will talk about social memory,

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.790
a heuristic known as foci of memory,

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.720
which I'll discuss what that is later,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.480
and how that affected
Buddhist terrace construction

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.120
as well as the 16th century
restoration of Angkor Wat,

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.230
which is probably the best known event

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.930
within Angkor's Theravada
Buddhist archeological record.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:14.763
And then finally,

00:04:14.763 --> 00:04:16.560
I'll discuss briefly how

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.890
mapping religious transition
in the archeological record

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.320
might take place.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.420
But the major thesis woven
to this presentation is that,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.970
Theravada Buddhism could
not have been successful

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.220
in its spread at Angkor without
a standardized reckoning

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.060
with Angkor's temple spaces.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.730
As a case study almost similar

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.840
to the adoption of Christianity in Rome,

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.280
with admittedly a less
pilfered archeological record.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.130
We asked the question of how
religious transition occurs

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.290
within established religious societies,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.140
and how can we map this through
the archeological record.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:51.880
The Angkorian or Khmer Empire
was a geopolitical entity,

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.960
which at its peak,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.660
encompass much of Thailand,
Laos, and Southern Vietnam

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.500
from the 9th century
to the 15th century CE,

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.430
although forms a single
episode within a continuous

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.310
uninterrupted Cambodian
culture and civilization,

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:08.240
which have existed since at
least the second millennium BC.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.500
The region of Angkor which
is shown on the right,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.400
was the beating heart and is
founded within a 1000 square

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.970
kilometer area of urban
and religious development,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.250
including temples, monasteries,
walls, embankments,

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:21.020
canals, road, and a massive

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.900
infrastructure of
undocumented wooden buildings.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.310
This area which is often seen

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:27.900
as a single urban entity
is purported to have been

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.050
the world's largest
pre-industrial settlement

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.310
with population estimates

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.023
ranging between 750,000 to a million.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:36.740
And essential to understanding

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.100
Angkor's development is the
establishment of Indic religion,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.160
both Hinduism and Buddhism in Cambodia.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.660
These traditions arrived
in Southeast Asia initially

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.930
via maritime trade or over land between

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:51.620
the 2nd century BCE and 5th century CE,

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.360
and were first recorded by Chinese traders

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.500
who noted that Cambodian policies
practiced both traditions

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.920
during, at least 5th to 7th century CE.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.320
Just to back up a moment,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.310
there are two schools of Buddhism practice

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.500
that were practiced
within Cambodian history.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.220
We're going to the Vajrayana Buddhism

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:11.550
on the table for a minute,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.810
but these are Theravada
Buddhism and Mahayana Buddhism.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:16.520
In the simplest terms,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.330
the major difference is the
latter's focus on collective

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.390
enlightenment to the
worship of bodhisattvas

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:22.770
or Buddhist saints,

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.970
and the construction of great works.

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.060
Theravada Buddhism,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.670
which is also known as
Sinhalese Buddhism is derived

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.180
from the teachings of the Pali Canon

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.620
from the Mahavihara
Monastery in Sri Lanka,

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.430
practiced there since the 2nd century BCE,

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:36.670
which were in turn,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.480
reinterpreted by populations
in Burma and Thailand

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.830
in the following millennia.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.950
This important point emphasizes
that only in scripture

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.160
are these traditions similar

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.740
to what is found in South Asian material.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.530
Southeast Asia created traditions

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.770
that were tied into
indigenous forms of rulership

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.410
and religious practices
surrounding the elements;

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.340
water, which often became
associated with the much

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.370
later idea of oriented despotism
and ancestral veneration.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.460
Therefore, this process of
hybridization of Indic culture

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.570
and indigenous rulerships

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.560
often known as the
Indianization of Southeast Asia

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.205
based off the work of the
prominent 20th century,

00:07:12.205 --> 00:07:14.450
epigrapher George Coedes and others.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.350
Important too is that this
occurred in (murmurs).

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:19.770
It was not a specific event adopting

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:20.880
Indic religion and culture,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.600
and more often occured between
polities in Southeast Asia,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.890
rather than between
South and Southeast Asia.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.160
For example, between
Thailand and Cambodia,

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.310
which was the spread
of Theravada Buddhism.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:34.610
Hindu and Buddhist worship,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.990
which is known collectively
as Brahman or Buddhism

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.153
due to their shared power
structures within Cambodia

00:07:39.153 --> 00:07:41.340
and other Southeast Asian polities,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.760
became a way to validate
and consolidate rule

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.280
over divided city states and princes.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.350
Southeast Asian polities
typically function

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:50.270
as centralized theocratic monarchies

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.050
ruled by a Chakravartin
or a universal ruler.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.970
The king was the wielder
of dhamma or moral law,

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.900
and is often also incarnated
as either Hindu deity,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.800
Shiva or Vishnu, or bodhisattva.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.320
Typically, Angkor Shiva was

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.123
the most commonly venerated deity,

00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:06.922
and the king's rule was manifested

00:08:06.922 --> 00:08:09.323
through the worship of the Shiva Lingam,

00:08:09.323 --> 00:08:11.690
a stone phallus which
combined with the Yoni,

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.840
a symbol of female fertility would bless

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.980
the farm lands and provide
a bountiful harvest.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.260
This king provided,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.680
or presided over a stratified
society of princes,

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.320
the Brahmanic elites,
caste artisans, merchants,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.340
soldiers, and other skilled laborers

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.870
and non-caste agrarian peasants.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.110
And it's often touted that only

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:32.300
the elites of any Southeast
Asian polity practice Hinduism.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.150
Although this is unclear as
only a small segment of society

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:38.550
was in fact incorporated within
the Varna or caste system.

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.870
The majority of society was agrarian

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.570
and historian David Chandler
notes that this caste system

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.970
divided society between
those who grew rice,

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.070
and those who did not.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:49.940
Within this model,

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.940
scholars have long read Southeast
Asian religious landscapes

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.200
as a direct reflection of statehood.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.650
Archeological analysis of
Southeast Asian statehood

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.950
have with us mainly focused
on the manufacturing

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.340
environments constructed
by religious power,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.440
namely the numerous Hindu

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.100
and Mahayana Buddhist
state temple complexes,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.150
as well as later Theravada
Buddhist monasteries,

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.620
as our researches illuminated.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.240
As well as their dedicatory
foundation inscriptions,

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.620
which were usually written in Sanskrit.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.160
And temples across of
any size or importance

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.500
across the Angkorian Empire
were commonly established

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.880
as cosmograms representing Mount Meru,

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.970
the home of the 33 gods of
Hinduism ruled by Lord Indra

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:27.270
and a sacred access Mundee

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.480
that represented one of
the 31 planes of existence.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.700
The central shrine
surrounded by four towers

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:33.660
or peaks of the mountain

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:36.320
as you can see here with Phnom Bakheng.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:37.620
How's the primary deity?

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:38.610
Either a Shiva Lingam

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:41.780
or a statue of either
the Vishu or a Buddha.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.602
Temples would also feature a (murmurs)

00:09:44.602 --> 00:09:46.320
or were fronted by a (murmurs)
known as a reservoir,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.740
representing the cosmic ocean,

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:49.573
and we're surrounded

00:09:49.573 --> 00:09:51.280
by urban developments including hydrology

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.043
and perishable wooden buildings,

00:09:55.251 --> 00:09:56.084
as also walls.

00:09:56.084 --> 00:09:57.900
Thus, these complexes are referred

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.410
to as civic ceremonial centers.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.940
The temple here, Phnom Bakheng,

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.150
was dedicated in 907 CE

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.700
by Angkor's first regional
king, Jayavarman the first.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.203
And the area of Angkor is
often known as Yashodharapura.

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:17.230
Temples also formed at the
center of their own economies.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.140
Damien Evans notes that as
microcosms of the King's capital,

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:23.010
village shrines and regional
temples across the empire

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.600
embodied the same political
and economic symbolism

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.180
as state temples on a smaller scale,

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.470
and essentially reinforced
the political and social

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.360
linkages within the network.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.180
And through his analysis of economic

00:10:35.263 --> 00:10:36.924
transactions found within epigraphy,

00:10:36.924 --> 00:10:38.635
most notably the inscription at Ta Prohm,

00:10:38.635 --> 00:10:40.920
which is the temple illustrated
here from the 11th century.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:44.070
Kenneth R Hall surmise that
temples controlled land,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.760
the labor on the land and
the lands productive output,

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.530
and thus formed foci of economic

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.780
systems and political mechanisms.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:53.180
Temples administered the
distribution of resources,

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:56.020
upheld social structures
through ritual and settlement,

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:57.710
featured indentured laborers

00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.570
tied to the temple known as Khon,

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.620
and villages were in fact tied
to temples administration.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.670
These were recorded in many inscriptions,

00:11:05.523 --> 00:11:06.510
for example, at the temple of Preah Khan,

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.810
which was constructed
in the late 12th century

00:11:09.657 --> 00:11:11.990
under the great King
Jayavarman the seventh,

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.360
which administered 5,324
villages, totalling 97,840 people,

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.900
and had an attached retinue of dancers,

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.533
priests, and temple staff.

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:23.360
The civic ceremonial center,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.070
which will be the focus of
today's talk is Angkor Thom,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.440
a three by three kilometer
walled in moated citadel

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.230
constructed in the late 12th century

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:32.050
during the rule of the great

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.960
Mahayana Buddhist King
Jahavarman the seventh,

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.100
as the centerpiece of this
complete reenvisioning

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.840
and transformation of
the Angkorian heartland.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.880
Angkor Thom was constructed
as a manifestation

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.100
of the churning of the ocean of milkiness.

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.100
And in this tale of Vedic mythology,

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:48.040
the asuras or demons
gained control of heaven,

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.559
forcing King Indra of
Mount Meru into exile.

00:11:50.559 --> 00:11:53.350
And these demons sought
the elixir of mortality

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.760
from the cosmic ocean for themselves.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.990
Vishnu who himself sought to
return Indra to his throne,

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.400
employed the devas, or
collectively the gods,

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.505
to help the asuras for a time,

00:12:03.505 --> 00:12:05.500
with promise to keep the elixir inhabit.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:06.840
In his act scene to balance

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.670
the universal forces of good and evil,

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.570
the devas and the asuras began
to churn the cosmic ocean

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.760
using the Naga Vasaki
as a churning mechanism.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.890
The asuras chose the
Mountain Mandara (murmurs)

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:17.910
as their churning post,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.370
which would create the elixir,

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:21.400
but the mountain began
to sink into the water,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.600
and Vishnu then transformed the...

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.520
Or it transformed into a turtle,

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.710
one of it's many avatars known as Kurma,

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.842
and stabilized the mountain.

00:12:29.842 --> 00:12:31.417
And one, in fact,

00:12:31.417 --> 00:12:32.660
one of the many Abhiseka
or coronation rites

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:33.970
to describe Angkorian Kings

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.360
was their drinking of
the elixir of immortality

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:37.963
as a validation to kingship.

00:12:39.626 --> 00:12:41.740
In reference to Angkor Thom's layout,

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.710
the devas and asuras still
align the city's five gateways

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.690
holding enormous Naga serpents,

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:48.360
and the animatic Bayon Temple,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.320
which we'll get to shortly,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:51.190
formed Mount Mandara (murmurs),

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.440
although excavations have not indicated

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.290
whether there is in fact a
giant turtle under the temple.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:59.257
As a walled urban area,

00:12:59.257 --> 00:13:01.760
Angkor Thom is also referred to as a city,

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.590
and is crisscrossed by an
orthogonal road grid alongside

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.450
a sophisticated hydraulic
system of canals and moats,

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:09.870
which dictated the construction
of new settlements.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.130
The construction of the
citadel also swallowed up

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.020
Angkor's earlier political hub,

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.120
which was centered on the late 9th century

00:13:17.416 --> 00:13:18.368
wild-walled palace complex,

00:13:18.368 --> 00:13:19.847
which is noted as a central zone

00:13:19.847 --> 00:13:21.130
in the diagram to the bottom right,

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.830
from which successive
Kings continued to rule.

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.030
The fifth of five arterial roads,

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:27.770
known as the Victory Gate road,

00:13:27.770 --> 00:13:29.970
originally ran eastward
from the royal palace

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:32.300
and linked the complex
to the great east borough

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.047
constructed by Angkor's first
king, Jayavarman the first.

00:13:35.047 --> 00:13:37.620
To get an extent of the
size of this borough,

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.683
it's 7.8 by 2.1 kilometers, large.

00:13:41.647 --> 00:13:44.160
Angkor Thom is also home, finally,

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.690
to the overwhelming majority
of archeological evidence

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:48.000
for the initial adoption

00:13:49.008 --> 00:13:50.313
and dissemination of Theravada Buddhism.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:53.930
Jayavarman the seventh
deserves a bit of background

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.286
as his brief subordination of Hinduism

00:13:56.286 --> 00:13:58.580
from Mahayana Buddhism is
relevant to our conversation

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.620
on the religious transition
in the archeological record.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.070
Jayavarman was reportedly
converted to this religion

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:06.840
by his first wife prior to
the beginning of his rule

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.300
and an enormous collection of
inscriptions from his reign

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.620
emphasize the bodily ills of the subjects

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.670
became the spiritual sufferings.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:15.580
The king therefore had
envisioned themselves

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.691
an incarnation of either the
bodhisattva Avalokitesvara,

00:14:18.691 --> 00:14:21.810
who represented compassion
or the Supreme Buddha,

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:24.006
who sat at the nexus of compassion wisdom.

00:14:24.006 --> 00:14:26.360
The face towers on many structures

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:27.870
constructed during his reign.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.970
As you can see in the bottom right here

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.250
are thought to represent

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:33.970
this overwhelming cult of personality.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:34.803
And in any case,

00:14:34.803 --> 00:14:37.170
Jayavarman sought to ease
the suffering of the subjects

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.457
through the construction of brick works

00:14:39.457 --> 00:14:40.440
and territorial consolidation.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.940
And in commencing the program,

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.320
both the specific projects
and the order in which

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.010
they were completed indeed correlate

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:49.040
with architectural styles and
building schemes undertaken

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.540
during the reign of
earlier Hindu monarchs.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.690
Using the substantial
translations and inscription

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:55.480
by George Coedes and others,

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:57.420
epigrapher Felix Stansley
was able to map out

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:58.600
a rhythm of three moments

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.230
royal construction across
multiple reign periods.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.250
And these would comprise, first,

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:04.457
the foundations of public interest

00:15:04.457 --> 00:15:06.420
and the renewal of state authority.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:07.253
The second,

00:15:07.253 --> 00:15:09.560
the completion of an
ancestral temple or temples.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.810
And third, the establishment
of the temple mountain

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.663
celebrating the lingam or royal cult.

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:16.100
For Jayavarman's rule,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.970
the first rhythm included a
vast infrastructural program,

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:21.130
including the repaving
of the empire's highways

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:23.570
alongside the construction of rest houses

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:25.300
and a whole infrastructure of hospitals

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.500
and fire shrines associated with him.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:30.160
The second was the
construction of two satellite

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:33.290
civic ceremonial centers,
Ta Prohm and Preah Khan,

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.283
dedicated to his mother and
father in central Angkor.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.340
And the third was a temple
was a mountain of his own,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.200
the Bayon Temple at the
center of Angkor Thom.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.326
This enigmatic temple,

00:15:44.326 --> 00:15:45.850
which is defined by 54 face towers

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:47.850
sits at the convergence
of four of the five

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.670
citadels architectural roads.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:50.990
And the temple again is both

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:54.410
an extension of the churning
of the ocean of milk myth,

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:58.270
as well as a more traditional
incarnation of Mount Meru.

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:00.640
Additionally, the Bayon was
constructed as a complex

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.540
architectural pantheon club
for (murmurs) Buddhist deities

00:16:04.551 --> 00:16:05.384
featuring shrines to Buddhas

00:16:05.384 --> 00:16:07.860
and bodhisattvas alongside Shiva, Vishnu,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.550
Rishi, Ganesh, and others.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:10.383
And it was no doubt,

00:16:10.383 --> 00:16:12.960
the spiritual heart of
Jayavarman's vision of empire,

00:16:14.344 --> 00:16:15.177
creating an umbrella of deities

00:16:15.177 --> 00:16:16.710
and thus the Hindu
elites who worship them,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:17.710
which he ruled over.

00:16:18.974 --> 00:16:22.690
The Bayon would indeed
maintain this position of power

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:24.108
at the center of Angkor

00:16:24.108 --> 00:16:25.697
until the abandonment of the citadel in

00:16:25.697 --> 00:16:28.098
the 15th century or believed abandonment.

00:16:28.098 --> 00:16:30.170
And as I'll discuss in a few slides,

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.530
the Bayon also formed an important focus

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:35.950
for Theravada Buddhist
construction within Angkor Thom.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.080
And as a result of the
enormous building campaign,

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:39.884
which defined his rule,

00:16:39.884 --> 00:16:41.420
Jayavarman's architectural
footprint is arguably

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.530
the largest of any Angkorian king,

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:46.110
but no distinct Angkorian
civic ceremonial centers

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.390
followed the foundation of Angkor Thom,

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.950
although the reason for this is disputed.

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.300
One suggestion by Damian
Evans is that Jayavarman

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:55.000
citadel and infrastructure already

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.430
encompassed so much of
Angkor's urban heartland,

00:16:57.430 --> 00:16:59.240
that it no longer made sense to construct

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:01.400
an entirely new capital from scratch.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.520
For instance, the creation
of a new urban center

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:06.080
would likely require the
retooling of any number of canals

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:07.980
within Angkor's hydraulic system,

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:09.790
a move that was likely unfeasible

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.930
due to a gradual population decline

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:14.280
and damage to Angkor's hydrology forecast

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.520
will begun during the 13th century,

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:17.620
which was primarily due

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:20.350
to climatic fluctuations
and over-exploitation,

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:22.210
although these are all
topics for another day

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.697
and a scholar far more
versed in this (indistinct).

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:28.070
The 13th century is also uniquely marked

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:30.071
with the return of the
royal call to Hinduism,

00:17:30.071 --> 00:17:32.370
thought to have been
spearheaded by an intensive

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:33.940
campaign of iconoclasm under

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:35.810
the reign of Jayavarman the eightth,

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:37.780
as you can see here on the right,

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.609
by this hacked out Buddha
image at Preah Khan.

00:17:40.609 --> 00:17:42.820
And Jayavarman the eighth
patronage of Hinduism

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.620
also came with some original
religious construction,

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:48.210
although not in the same
capacity as the earlier monarchs.

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:49.620
And the last ancient within Angkor,

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.920
the Mangalartha dedicated to Vishnu,

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:54.610
was constructed in 1295 CE.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:55.680
Although it's unclear whether

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:58.094
Mahinda Buddhism is continuously
practiced after this period

00:17:58.094 --> 00:18:00.060
or whether it was simply an episode

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:01.620
within Angkor's history of corresponding

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.220
with the Jayavarman the seventh.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.720
During the 13th century, if
not even during the late 12th,

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:10.610
a third religion began to
gain a foothold in Cambodia,

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.430
in part due to Jayavarman's
consolidation of the Western

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:14.960
border of his empire.

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:17.810
This was Theravada Buddhism,
which as I previously noted,

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.540
is derived from Southeast Asia

00:18:20.406 --> 00:18:22.273
interpretations of Sinhalese Buddhism.

00:18:22.273 --> 00:18:24.240
Oregionally analysis of the Dvaravati

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.190
material culture of Thailand by scholars,

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:28.500
such as Stephen Murphy
suggests that Buddhism

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:31.400
was practiced in the region
directly proximate to Cambodia,

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:34.320
at least by the 6th
century CE if not earlier.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.730
These Dvaravati polities notably those

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.350
on the Khorat plateau of
central eastern Thailand

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:40.670
were almost always situated

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:43.600
on the frontiers of several
larger imperial geo bodies,

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.227
for example, the Burmese of Pogon

00:18:45.227 --> 00:18:47.160
and the Khmer of Angkor.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.920
The nature of the medieval
Southeast Asian state is often

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.640
characterized as a
modular or cosmic circle.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.710
And this is so hyper and
hypothesized by scholars

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:56.953
such as Stanley Tebana and O.W Walters

00:18:56.953 --> 00:18:58.850
to emplace the relationship of kings

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:01.420
in a state of balanced
divine power radiating

00:19:02.265 --> 00:19:03.164
from a concentric point

00:19:03.164 --> 00:19:04.530
across a highly spiritualized territory,

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.500
which we might call an empire.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:07.333
In this model,

00:19:07.333 --> 00:19:09.430
the borders of Southeast Asian empires,

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:10.450
I guess we could call them that,

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:12.180
continuously waxed and waned

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:13.803
constantly emphasizing the center.

00:19:13.803 --> 00:19:15.690
But very little focus has been given

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.230
to the cultural transmission,

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:19.890
which occurred in this
often ignored frontier

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.170
due to archeological and
religious nationalism,

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:24.253
cementing history within modern borders.

00:19:27.188 --> 00:19:28.705
Although Burmese sources indicate

00:19:28.705 --> 00:19:30.520
that Jayavarman the seventh's
son, Tom Melinda, in fact,

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:32.640
reportedly visited the Mahavihara

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.140
with Burmese monks in 1180 CE.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:37.270
The first account of
Theravada Buddhist practice

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:40.360
in material cultured Angkor
comes from the 1296 CE

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:41.920
writings of Zhou Daguan

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:44.390
entitled, "The Customs of Cambodia".

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:47.050
Zhou was an envoy sent
from China by (murmurs)

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:48.700
to survey and document the regions

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:50.760
that China saw to be under the dominion,

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.560
and also possibly because a new
king, Indravarman the third,

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.670
had been crowned a year earlier.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:57.220
Zhou arrived at Angkor Thom

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:58.500
and through a somewhat fragmented

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.750
perception of foreign customs and culture,

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:03.640
observed as many as three
practicing religions.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:05.790
Brahmans, which he named specifically,

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:07.660
worshipers venerating a block of stone

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.640
would possibly be Shiva worshipers

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:11.910
and Buddhists whose
monks wore saffron robes

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.920
and worship large images of
the Buddha in grooved halls,

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:16.840
the first mention of
any structure resembling

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:19.380
what we found in the archeological record.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:20.700
Zhou also importantly notes

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.610
that every family practiced Buddhism,

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:24.690
which suggests that the
construction of the Mangalartha,

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.075
a year earlier, was a product of just

00:20:27.075 --> 00:20:29.186
one of the traditions of practice being,

00:20:29.186 --> 00:20:30.748
or being practiced at Angkor

00:20:30.748 --> 00:20:32.000
rather than some
overnight flip the switch,

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.490
as it somewhat suggested
earlier scholarship.

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.410
It's likely that Theravaaa
had been there for a time.

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:39.000
The King Indravarman the third

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.720
was also a practicing Buddhist,

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:41.800
and Zhou notes,

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.840
generally when leading a
return into the palace,

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.830
he had to visit a small gold pagoda

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:47.250
with a Buddha in front of it.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.180
Onlookers all had to kneel down

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.930
and touch their head on the ground.

00:20:51.875 --> 00:20:52.830
Theravada Buddhist practice

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:53.740
after this point

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:55.410
is thought to gradually gain ground

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:57.450
on the remaining Hindu
traditions as a religion

00:20:57.450 --> 00:21:00.280
widely practiced across
all social classes.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:01.610
And while hierarchy indeed

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:03.973
continued to exist within the ritual

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:05.743
performed and practiced in monasteries,

00:21:05.743 --> 00:21:06.970
rulership appears to be less tied

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:09.064
to connection with Hindu dates.

00:21:09.064 --> 00:21:10.260
The epigraphic language in fact

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:12.000
shifted from Sanskrit to Pali.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:15.040
The canon language assemblies
Buddhism in the 14th century,

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:17.830
and an inscription exists from 1309 CE,

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:20.690
at Svay Kok Chek Temple
in Northwestern, Cambodia,

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:22.560
which describes the King's application

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.100
for the life of a bhikkhu or a monk,

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:25.920
which seems unprecedented.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.180
The king would typically
have died in his position

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:30.118
if he wasn't overthrown or killed.

00:21:30.118 --> 00:21:32.800
The last inscription dedicated to Shiva

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.740
appears on the Bayon from 1327 CE,

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:38.330
which is no worthy as
much for its dedication

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:40.950
to a Hindu king as its
inscription on a monument,

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:43.520
which may have kept its
significance as a pantheon,

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:46.393
but certainly remained
emblematic of royal authority.

00:21:50.011 --> 00:21:52.070
Theravada Buddhism also
introduced a new material culture

00:21:53.313 --> 00:21:54.170
comprising of new religious
and monastic spaces

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:56.053
called Vihara or prayer halls,

00:21:56.996 --> 00:21:57.918
which are derived,

00:21:57.918 --> 00:21:59.213
likely derive from those founded

00:21:59.213 --> 00:22:00.046
within Buddhist polities
in Northern Thailand,

